clock_nanosleep as POSIX and Linux have it. mlibc's asserted that the
clock was CLOCK_REALTIME or CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so CLOCK_BOOTTIME or any
other ended the program with SIGABRT; it returned -1 and set errno where
POSIX returns the error number and leaves errno alone, so the usual
`while(clock_nanosleep(...) == EINTR)` loop ended at the first signal;
it took any time, and wrote `rem` for an interrupted absolute sleep.
Now: REALTIME, MONOTONIC, BOOTTIME, TAI and the process's CPU time are
slept on; the clocks Linux has but drives no timers with (MONOTONIC_RAW,
the COARSE and the ALARM clocks) are ENOTSUP, a thread's CPU time and
unknown clocks EINVAL, as Linux answers, checked on Linux 6.8; a time
that is not one is EINVAL; a sleep waits until the clock reads the end,
looking again after each wait (so a sleep on a clock that ran slower than
the wait goes on), and a sleep on the process's CPU time waits at most
the share of what is left that every CPU together could use. A relative
time past the clock's last is a sleep without end. Meant for upstream.

